t disease (GVHD) following hematopoietic cell transplantation. Key entities include various forms of GVHD, the transplantation procedure itself, and the nutritional support required for patients undergoing these treatments. The relationships among these entities highlight the complexities and risks involved in managing post-transplant complications.\n\n## Graft-versus-host disease as a critical post-transplant complication\n\nGraft-versus-host disease (GVHD) is a significant complication that can arise after hematopoietic cell transplantation, where the transplanted cells attack the recipient's body. This condition can lead to severe morbidity and mortality, making it a critical focus for medical professionals. The acute form of GVHD typically presents within the first few weeks post-transplant, affecting the skin, liver, and intestines, while chronic GVHD can develop later, requiring ongoing management and care. The relationship between GVHD and the transplantation process underscores the importance of monitoring patients closely for signs of this condition. [Data: Entities (13736, 13781, 2333, 19644); Relationships (19644, 19641, 7048)]\n\n## Nutritional support as essential for recovery\n\nNutritional support is crucial for patients undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation, as they often experience malnutrition due to the treatment and its side effects. Both parenteral and enteral nutrition may be necessary to ensure adequate caloric intake and support recovery. Patients with acute or chronic GVHD may require specialized nutritional interventions to manage their condition effectively. The relationships between nutritional support and the transplantation process highlight the need for a multidisciplinary approach to patient care, integrating nutritional management with medical treatment. [Data: Entities (1647, 2149); Relationships (4820, 6762, 2795, 4818, 4817)]\n\n## Corticosteroids as a primary treatment for GVHD\n\nCorticosteroids are a cornerstone in the 