sights into a patient's health status. For instance, the evaluation of prealbumin levels and other biochemical parameters can indicate the effectiveness of nutritional support and guide further interventions. The systematic use of these markers is vital for ensuring comprehensive care for patients, particularly those with complex health needs. [Data: Entities (7160, 7161), Relationships (4296, 4299, 4294)]\n\n## Nutritional Assessment in Specific Conditions\n\nNutritional assessment is particularly critical for managing patients with specific conditions such as cirrhosis, cancer, and those undergoing hematopoietic cell transplants. For example, in patients with cirrhosis, nutritional assessment helps prevent malnutrition, which can exacerbate their condition. Similarly, for oncology patients, regular evaluations are essential to determine their risk of malnutrition and to tailor dietary interventions accordingly. This targeted approach underscores the importance of nutritional assessments in improving health outcomes for these vulnerable populations. [Data: Relationships (4288, 4303, 4301)]\n\n## Guidelines and Protocols for Nutritional Assessment\n\nOrganizations like ASPEN provide guidelines on nutritional assessment for patients at risk of malnutrition. These guidelines are crucial for healthcare providers to ensure that assessments are conducted systematically and effectively. The integration of these protocols into clinical practice enhances the quality of care for hospitalized patients, ensuring that their nutritional needs are met and monitored throughout their treatment. [Data: Relationships (4295, 4292)]", "# Desnutrición and Oncological Patients\n\nThe community focuses on the critical issue of desnutrición (malnutrition) affecting various patient populations, particularly oncological patients. The entities are interconnected through their relationships, highlighting the importance of nutritional support and interventions in managing health outcomes for th